@gregmarr Yes, one: MessagePack. Array 32 and map 32 (`0xDD`/`0xDF`) read a `uint32_t` length and passed it straight on, so with a 32-bit `size_t` a length of `0xFFFFFFFF` became `start_array(unknown_size())`. That skipped the `max_size()` check and ended in a misleading "unexpected end of input" instead of an excessive-size error, and a custom SAX handler was told the size was unknown. It was never accepted as valid, unlike the UBJSON case. Fixed in `da6d21ae24`: both now go through the CBOR length check, renamed `get_container_size()`, which rejects a length equal to `unknown_size()`. `unit-32bit.cpp` has a test next to the CBOR one.

The other places are already covered. CBOR's 32-bit lengths (`0x9A`/`0xBA`) already went through that check. BJData `m`/`M` counts are handled by this PR, and the ndarray dimension product already rejects an exact `npos`. UBJSON `l` is signed and tops out at 2^31-1. BSON has no element counts, only int32 byte lengths. String and binary lengths (MessagePack str/bin 32, BSON) have no sentinel value.

*(Written by Claude Code on behalf of @nlohmann.)*
